>Oh, wait, but then this group would be boring.  No one will post anymore.

Actually, it wouldn't be such a bad idea to have some kind of a macro
language in NetHack. A powerful one, that is, so you can build commands
like "Goto altar" under one key. Which would take you across several
level, automatically chosing the best route (using teleport if
possible), avoiding minor hazards. The macro should abort if a major
problem is detected - so you end up in manual mode.

Just think of the possibilities. The quest for the ultimate polypile
macro. The "stupid deads" arising from typos in macro programs. David
Hairstons end games macro collection. The battle macros from xanth -
press M-b and indicate the monster you want dead. I see no shortage in
postings comming in this group. OK, it will be renamed comp.source.nethack,
especially after the macro controlled monsters and artifacts are
introduced. Academic status will be reached when Knuth releases nhWeb
and starts on a seven year project to design a macro which will play
the complete game for you (and write the first thousand decimals of PI
all over the dungeon). But I'm digressing.

What I mean is that you can put parts of your own strategy in a macro,
just to go through the boring bits of it a bit faster. And, of course,
macro controlled monsters (and artifacts), with their own goals and
strategies, would be great too. Want a monster to behave differently?
Just adapt its macro program. In fact separate groups could work on
different kinds of monsters, allowing more people to work on the
development of nethack at the same time.

Now, can this be implemented? The answer is, of course:
(SYSTEM==MS-DOS) ? "NO" : "YES". Emacs LISP shows it can be done. What
is needed is a programming language with primitives to interface with
nethack to be build into nethack. The best way to go is to build a nice
high level programming language which can be build into any
application.  The application just has to start the interpreter and
provide it with a set of extra primitives suitable for that
application. An example of thsi method is the REXX language under the
VM operating system, which is build into many programs. Not only is it
the shell language of VM, it is also used to program editors, debuggers
etc.

Maybe a GNU project to develop such a language can be started. It would
really be a nice addition to UNIX, both for the programmers (which can
build a powerful macro language into every program at little cost) and
for the users (which can reprogram everything - and all of it in the
same language).
